having a good open source posture is

1339
00:48:08,240 --> 00:48:10,030
very attractive to developers arriving

1340
00:48:10,040 --> 00:48:11,710
at your organization. It's kind of like

1341
00:48:11,720 --> 00:48:13,750
a mark of the quality of the development

1342
00:48:13,760 --> 00:48:15,630
work going on in your firm. And one of

1343
00:48:15,640 --> 00:48:18,110
the big ones is that if you don't allow

1344
00:48:18,120 --> 00:48:19,910
staff to contribute to open source

1345
00:48:19,920 --> 00:48:22,510
software, and then they find an issue in

1346
00:48:22,520 --> 00:48:24,870
that software, an open source component

1347
00:48:24,880 --> 00:48:26,110
that they want to use, what they'll do

1348
00:48:26,120 --> 00:48:28,190
is they'll take a copy of that software,

1349
00:48:28,200 --> 00:48:30,430
they'll stick it in their internal

1350
00:48:30,440 --> 00:48:32,230
source control, and then they'll make

1351
00:48:32,240 --> 00:48:33,550
the change locally, and then they'll

1352
00:48:33,560 --> 00:48:35,550
support that from that day forwards

1353
00:48:35,560 --> 00:48:37,710
forever. That's both expensive, so it's

1354
00:48:37,720 --> 00:48:39,670
got financial risk associated with it.

1355
00:48:39,680 --> 00:48:41,270
That developer is now wasting a lot of

1356
00:48:41,280 --> 00:48:44,310
time integrating upstream dependencies,

1357
00:48:44,320 --> 00:48:46,830
but it's also a security risk because

1358
00:48:46,840 --> 00:48:49,150
the chances are they're not keeping that

1359
00:48:49,160 --> 00:48:51,390
branch up to date with what's going on

1360
00:48:51,400 --> 00:48:53,470
in the public repo, either. So, there's

1361
00:48:53,480 --> 00:48:55,870
a whole bunch of risks there if you

1362
00:48:55,880 --> 00:48:57,470
don't contribute to open source

1363
00:48:57,480 --> 00:48:59,350
software. But then there's also a whole

1364
00:48:59,360 --> 00:49:01,790
bunch of risks if you do. So, one of the

1365
00:49:01,800 --> 00:49:04,550
key ones that firms really worry about

1366
00:49:04,560 --> 00:49:07,550
in regulated industries like finance is

1367
00:49:07,560 --> 00:49:11,390
that some, you know, hapless developer

1368
00:49:11,400 --> 00:49:14,270
will accidentally share some private

1369
00:49:14,280 --> 00:49:15,950
client information in a GitHub

1370
00:49:15,960 --> 00:49:17,870
repository, or they'll paste it onto

1371
00:49:17,880 --> 00:49:20,270
Facebook or or whatever. And you usually

1372
00:49:20,280 --> 00:49:22,310
the firewall policies in an organization

1373
00:49:22,320 --> 00:49:24,190
will prevent access to these sites and

1374
00:49:24,200 --> 00:49:26,790
prevent people from you know, doing HTTP

1375
00:49:26,800 --> 00:49:28,670
post requests and things like this in

1376
00:49:28,680 --> 00:49:31,670
order to minimize that risk. So, there's

1377
00:49:31,680 --> 00:49:34,870
this huge worry around accidentally

1378
00:49:34,880 --> 00:49:37,510
posting code that you didn't want to

1379
00:49:37,520 --> 00:49:41,070
release, or PII data, or materially

1380
00:49:41,080 --> 00:49:44,110
sensitive information, company secrets,

1381
00:49:44,120 --> 00:49:47,350
secret keys, server details, all this

1382
00:49:47,360 --> 00:49:48,750
sort of stuff. You've got to be careful

1383
00:49:48,760 --> 00:49:51,270
with all that. And so, just opening the

1384
00:49:51,280 --> 00:49:54,070
floodgates and allowing any employee to

1385
00:49:54,080 --> 00:49:56,390
use their work computer to contribute to

1386
00:49:56,400 --> 00:49:59,230
open source is a huge risk. And so,

1387
00:49:59,240 --> 00:50:00,990
Finos actually provides tools like Git

1388
00:50:01,000 --> 00:50:03,270
Proxy, which is like a proxy that sits

1389
00:50:03,280 --> 00:50:06,950
between your staff computer and then

1390
00:50:06,960 --> 00:50:10,350
GitHub or GitLab and allows for a like a

1391
00:50:10,360 --> 00:50:13,030
four-eyed review to occur to anything

1392
00:50:13,040 --> 00:50:14,470
you want to push to the open source

1393
00:50:14,480 --> 00:50:16,470
project. So, I guess what I'm saying is

1394
00:50:16,480 --> 00:50:18,430
that you don't have to just accept these

1395
00:50:18,440 --> 00:50:20,070
risks as they are. You can actually use

1396
00:50:20,080 --> 00:50:22,310
tools to manage those risks and and make

1397
00:50:22,320 --> 00:50:24,990
that open source contribution option

1398
00:50:25,000 --> 00:50:27,550
much more acceptable from a risk profile

1399
00:50:27,560 --> 00:50:30,110
point of view. Does that make sense?

1400
